[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Styling and CSS Support in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript provides robust support for CSS to style your mobile applications. While it does not use a standard web DOM, it implements a rich subset of CSS properties tailored for native UI rendering across iOS and Android."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Global and Component-Level Styles"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can apply styles globally across your entire NativeScript application or scope them to specific components. Global styles are typically defined in the app.css or app.scss file located in the root of your project."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"/* app.css */
.page {
  background-color: #f4f4f4;
}

Button {
  background-color: #007acc;
  color: #ffffff;
  font-size: 16;
  border-radius: 4;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Supported CSS Properties"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript supports a familiar subset of CSS properties, allowing you to control layout, typography, and colors easily. Key supported properties include:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Colors and backgrounds: color, background-color, background-image"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Typography: font-family, font-size, font-weight, font-style, text-align"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dimensions and Box Model: width, height, margin, padding, border-width, border-color, border-radius"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Flexbox Layout in CSS"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript has built-in support for Flexbox, making it straightforward to build responsive layouts that adapt dynamically to different screen sizes and orientations on both iOS and Android."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":".container {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Platform-Specific CSS"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need different styling rules for iOS versus Android. NativeScript allows you to target specific platforms using specialized file naming conventions or CSS selectors."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"/* Target only iOS */
.ios Button {
  font-family: "San Francisco";
}

/* Target only Android */
.android Button {
  font-family: "Roboto";
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By leveraging CSS in NativeScript, web developers can apply familiar styling concepts to native mobile applications. Utilize global stylesheets, component scoping, and Flexbox to create polished cross-platform user interfaces."}]
